# R8 follow-up — the corpus run at the shipped `auto` worker count (2026-11-02)

R8 (`docs/design-m2-thread-policy.md`) changed the default from llama.cpp's compile-time four to
`min(cpus, max(min(cpus, 4), floor(0.8 × cpus)))`, which is **twelve** on this host. §14's corpus rows
(16,161 chunks/hour) were taken at four and stay that way: `crates/np-core/tests/local_corpus.rs` pins four in
`run_pass`, and the auto count runs as its own case, `the_corpus_pass_under_the_auto_worker_count_is_measured`.

Commands (one process per configuration, `--test-threads=1`; the two configurations are separate invocations so neither
one's page cache or CPU state contaminates the other):

    NP_TEST_MODEL_PATH=/home/agent/models/embeddinggemma-2-Q8_0.gguf \
      cargo test --locked -p np-core --features local-model --test local_corpus \
        the_corpus_pass_under_the_auto_worker_count -- --nocapture --test-threads=1      # run twice

    … --test local_corpus a_first_pass_over_a_corpus_is_measured_and_projected …          # four-worker control, same day

Host: 16-core x86_64 Linux, CPU-only, `MemTotal: 7597988 kB`. Artifact:
`/home/agent/models/embeddinggemma-2-Q8_0.gguf`, 309,855,456 B, sha256
`2188ac1deca4b77dffefd603c2776a9d76d9d74ec01841392982ebb840b09135`. llama.cpp pinned tag v0.8.0, `GGML_OPENMP=OFF`.
Worker counts below are printed by the harness (`workers=` on every row), not asserted from the config file.

**Three runs, in the order they were taken.** §8 asks for ≥2 and forbids quoting a lucky number; the four-worker control
is here because a ratio between two days is a claim about the host as much as about the threads — and it holds: today's
four-worker pass (456.43 s, 16,153 chunks/hour) reproduces the 2026-10-31 pair (456.20 / 456.16 s, 16,161 / 16,163) to
within 0.06 %, so this machine did not simply get faster.

| Figure | four workers (control, today) | auto = 12, run 1 | auto = 12, run 2 | ratio 12/4 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| steady wall time for 2,048 chunks | 456.43 s | 181.41 s | 180.97 s | **2.516× / 2.522×** |
| tokens/s (sample-derived) | 154.5 | 388.6 | 389.6 | 2.515× / 2.522× |
| chunks/hour (exact counts) | 16,153 | 40,642 | 40,741 | 2.516× / 2.522× |
| `VmRSS` drift across the pass (KiB) | +1,556 | +1,556 | +1,556 | — |
| peak `VmHWM` (KiB) | 447,288 | 446,392 | 446,440 | −0.2 % (indistinguishable) |
| cold start: load + one decode (s) | 0.42 | 0.40 | 0.39 | — |
| notes fitting one 4 h evening | ~64,600 | ~162,500 | ~162,900 | — |

**What this does not change.** The `fits_evening` verdicts: at four workers the projection's largest cohort (20,000 notes)
already fitted in 1.24 h of the 4 h threshold, so the answer was "yes" before and is "yes" now — what the threads buy is
headroom (0.49 h instead of 1.24 h), not a threshold that previously failed. The cohort solve (§14's fixed-cost split,
31.1 ms + 5.53 ms/token) is still a four-worker measurement: it is about per-decode overhead and SQLite, and this case
deliberately does not re-run it. Peak RSS moving ~0.2 % is not a contradiction of D13's "+6.6 MiB for twelve workers" —
that was mid-run `VmRSS` on a single-chunk probe against the same probe at four; this is whole-process `VmHWM` dominated by
the 1,104 MiB compute reservation, and the honest statement is "no measurable change at this granularity".

